---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SiteHeader from '../../components/SiteHeader.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../components/SiteFooter.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="How I build trainings with AI — Field notes"
  description="From a client's wish to slides in the room: the spine, the tools, and where I still step in myself."
>
  <SiteHeader solid />

  <main id="main" class="page">
    <article class="shell note-article">
      <p class="eyebrow"><a href={`${base}field-notes/`}>Field notes</a> · Nov 2026</p>
      <h1>How I build trainings with AI</h1>
      <p class="page-lede">From a client's wish to slides in the room — and the eight minutes that only work because the spine exists.</p>

      <div class="prose-block">
        <p>
          Two days before a workshop I asked the client for a screenshot, and it showed that half of my training wouldn't
          work. Eight minutes and one sentence to Claude Code later, it did. Those eight minutes are what this note is
          about, but most of the real work happened long before them.
        </p>
        <p>
          I help enterprises upskill their people in AI. A lot of my work is trainings: a concept, HTML slides, prompt
          handouts, exercises, and videos for self-paced learning. This is how I build them today, and where I still step
          in myself.
        </p>

        <h2>My setup</h2>
        <div class="prose-table-wrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Work</th>
                <th>Tool</th>
                <th>Model or setting</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>Clarify what the client wants</td>
                <td>Matt Pocock's grilling skill, used as is, in Claude Code</td>
                <td>Claude Opus 5.5, high effort</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Keep decisions</td>
                <td>A spec and ADRs as Markdown in <code>./docs</code>, linked from <code>AGENTS.md</code></td>
                <td>Claude Opus 5.5, high effort</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Build slides and handouts</td>
                <td>Claude Code</td>
                <td>Claude Opus 5.5, medium effort</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Bulk changes</td>
                <td>Voice: macOS dictation, Claude Code voice mode, or GitHub Copilot voice</td>
                <td>Copilot voice runs a local model</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Design tweaks</td>
                <td>VS Code's integrated browser — select an HTML element and comment on it</td>
                <td>Claude Opus 5.5, low to medium effort</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Word-level fixes</td>
                <td>My own inline editor</td>
                <td>No AI</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Verification</td>
                <td>Linting, plus a <code>/checkawesome</code> skill for some offerings</td>
                <td>Claude Opus 5.5, medium effort</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Slides to the client</td>
                <td>Plain HTML, exported to PDF by Claude</td>
                <td>Claude Opus 5.5, medium effort</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Self-paced videos</td>
                <td>HeyGen MCP connector in Claude Code, HyperFrames HTML overlay</td>
                <td>HeyGen stock avatar with lip-sync</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Quizzes and packaging</td>
                <td>Claude Code, SCORM or the LMS's own format</td>
                <td>Built from content plus learning outcomes</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Fixes after delivery</td>
                <td>GitHub issues</td>
                <td>Me, for now</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Cost</td>
                <td>Claude Pro</td>
                <td>n/a</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2>Decision 1: let a skill grill me before anything gets built</h2>
        <p>
          A new training starts with a client and a fuzzy wish. Before I write a slide, I let Matt Pocock's grilling skill
          interview me about what the client actually wants. I use it as it is, in Claude Code with Opus 5.5 on high. The
          answers go into a spec, and Claude records the decisions as ADRs, short Markdown decision records in
          <code>./docs</code>. <code>AGENTS.md</code> describes the folder structure and links to those files, so every
          session starts from the same decisions.
        </p>

        <h2>Decision 2: go straight to building</h2>
        <p>
          I rarely split the spec into tasks. I need to iterate fast, so most of the time I go straight to building.
          Sometimes I ask the agent for a task list with checkboxes it ticks off as it goes. My first prompt for the
          workshop below was this:
        </p>
        <pre><code>in this repo we will design slides based on the training concept, the medium for the slides is not PPT but html.</code></pre>
        <p>That's all. The prompt can be short because the context isn't in the prompt.</p>

        <h2>Decision 3: let it run, because it has a spine</h2>
        <p>
          I let Claude Code run on its own. It can do good work because it has what I call the spine: the spec, the ADRs,
          <code>AGENTS.md</code> and the skills working together. Others call it a factory or a system. I run Opus 5.5 on
          high effort, because content work needs it to get the taste right.
        </p>
        <p>
          Building the spine is where most of my time goes. Encoding taste and judgment is hard, and it only gets better
          through iteration.
        </p>

        <h2>Decision 4: three ways to steer, at the same time</h2>
        <p>While the agent works, I steer it in three ways:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>By voice</strong> for bulk changes that run in the background. GitHub Copilot's voice mode even runs
            on a local model.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>In VS Code's integrated browser</strong> for design: I select an HTML element and comment on it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>In my own inline editor</strong> for tiny fixes, like a German word the translation got slightly
            wrong. I don't want to talk to an agent about one word.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          I keep rebuilding that inline editor for every project. The next step is a blueprint that bundles the slides,
          the editor, the integrated browser and the skills in one place.
        </p>

        <h2>Decision 5: write taste down so the agent can check itself</h2>
        <p>
          Linting catches the mechanical problems. For taste, I write criteria into a skill. For one of my offerings,
          <code>/checkawesome</code> checks the content against rules like these:
        </p>
        <pre><code>1. Simplicity: no more than 3–4 phases, formats or domains.
4. No AI slop: factual, short and specific. No slogans, triads, filler or em-dash constructions.
6. Clear focus, no scope creep: concise and compact, with explicit exclusions and a change request rule.
7. Learning objectives before content: always lead with drafted learning objectives, never with learning content.
8. Assumptions, not questions: state open points as our assumptions. Questions invite scope creep.
9. One place for timing: all dates and months live on the Zeitplan slide only.
12. Consistent and verified: every number matches across all slides, checked in Excel with formulas (37 of 37).
13. Terse writing: short sentences, applied to the deck and to the emails.</code></pre>
        <p>Rules 7 to 13 weren't in the first version. I added them along the way, every time something annoyed me.</p>

        <h2>Decision 6: video comes from the same repo</h2>
        <p>
          For self-paced trainings I produce videos with lip-sync avatars, and I don't open HeyGen myself. It starts with
          a CSV of learning outcomes. From that, Claude builds a storyboard, then writes the script. Claude then creates
          the video with a HeyGen stock avatar through the HeyGen MCP connector in Claude Code. On top of the avatar sits
          a HyperFrames HTML overlay, which Claude generates from the script, the slides and a brand
          <code>design.md</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
          I review only the storyboard. Once that's right, I let Claude take the script and the video the rest of the
          way.
        </p>

        <h2>Decision 7: labs get two test runs</h2>
        <p>
          My trainings include step-by-step lab guides with practical exercises. Labs are the critical piece, because
          they have to work in the room. First Claude works through the lab as a persona from the target audience, taken
          from the spec, and reports back to me. Then I work through it myself.
        </p>

        <h2>Decision 8: quizzes and packaging come last</h2>
        <p>
          To check knowledge, Claude writes quizzes from the finished content combined with the learning outcomes. When
          content development is done, Claude also packages everything, as SCORM or whatever format the client's learning
          management system (LMS) needs. The work then reaches the client either through me or through their LMS.
        </p>

        <h2>A real example: “Mit KI lernen”</h2>
        <p>
          “Mit KI lernen” (AI as your learning companion) is a 55-minute workshop. Participants make Microsoft Copilot
          their learning companion, using a start prompt with four parts (goal, context, source, expectations) and five
          techniques for checking Copilot's answers.
        </p>
        <p>
          The concept, version 2, was more ambitious: in 60 minutes everyone would build a learning companion in a
          Copilot Notebook with three prompts. Then Copilot changed. Notebooks became Workspaces, and the participants
          had no access to them. I found out because I asked the client for a screenshot two days before the session.
        </p>
        <p>
          About half the course had to change, and the rework took eight minutes. I told Claude Code:
        </p>
        <pre><code>add a basic path using only copilot chat.</code></pre>
        <p>
          The new basic path is one start prompt plus an uploaded document. The three-prompt notebook setup became an
          optional advanced path for people who have access. The 19 slides went to the client as a PDF.
        </p>

        <h2>Honest caveats</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            I didn't run <code>/checkawesome</code> on this workshop. It came later, for another offering.
          </li>
          <li>The platform I teach changes under me. No spec catches that. A screenshot from the client did.</li>
          <li>
            After delivery, I fix things myself through GitHub issues. An automation in the cloud should pick those up
            soon, but it doesn't yet.
          </li>
          <li>
            I'm more hands-on than I want to be. Getting hands-off needs a stronger spine: taste and quality written down
            as rules, scripts and skills that let the agent verify its own work.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What stays mine</h2>
        <p>
          Before a session I still scroll through every slide myself and dictate whatever catches my eye. What catches it
          is almost always content: whether this actually fits this client and these people. For videos, the storyboard
          is mine. For labs, the final run-through is mine. Checking the client's real setup is mine too. The agent builds
          fast. Deciding what's worth building, and noticing when the ground has moved, stays with me.
        </p>
        <p>
          A friend who read a draft of this put it more bluntly than I would. Reworking half a course two days before a
          training, so it fits the client exactly, doesn't work without AI. It also doesn't work without a lot of
          practice with AI. But my real edge isn't either of those. It's that I know what this client needs to hear in
          the room. Someone else with the same tools can't copy that.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="note-back">
        <a href={`${base}field-notes/`}>← All field notes</a>
        ·
        <a href={`${base}how-i-work/`}>How I work</a>
        ·
        <a href={`${base}speaking/`}>Speaking &amp; teaching</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>

  <SiteFooter />
</BaseLayout>
